>
  <section id="short-answer">
    <h2>Short answer: square footage is not enough information</h2>
    <p>Two houses with 2,000 square feet of conditioned space can have different backup requirements because one may use gas heat and city water while another uses a heat pump, electric resistance backup heat, electric water heating, and a well pump. Even similar homes can have different central-air conditioner models, motor-start requirements, and outage priorities.</p>
    <p>We do not map a floor area to a generator size or offer a calculator. Instead, use this page to identify what changes the load, collect the equipment data, and discuss it with a qualified local professional.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="profiles">
    <h2>Three 2,000-square-foot homes with different load questions</h2>
    <div class="not-prose mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-[#d9e0ea]">
      <table class="mobile-card-table min-w-[700px] w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
        <caption class="sr-only">Illustrative load-planning differences for homes with identical floor area</caption>
        <thead class="bg-[#edf3fb] text-primary"><tr><th scope="col" class="p-3">Illustrative home profile</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">Information that may matter</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">What a professional must verify</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-gray-800">
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Gas furnace, city water, one central AC</th><td class="p-3">AC model and starting data; furnace fan; refrigeration; selected kitchen and lighting loads.</td><td class="p-3">Which circuits must run together, HVAC startup, and whether loads can be sequenced or managed.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Heat pump with electric auxiliary heat, electric water heater</th><td class="p-3">Compressor operation, auxiliary strip stages, water heating, and other large electric loads.</td><td class="p-3">Actual controls and nameplate demand; whether auxiliary loads can be safely excluded or controlled during backup.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3">Gas heat, well water, sump pump, central AC</th><td class="p-3">Pump motor starts, AC starts, and which loads may overlap.</td><td class="p-3">Pump and HVAC model data, start sequence, transfer equipment, and load-management design.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4">These profiles are examples of questions to investigate, not measured homes or capacity recommendations.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="nameplate-data">
    <h2>What to collect before requesting a quote</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Heating and cooling system manufacturer, model, voltage, full-load amperage, and starting/locked-rotor information if published.</li>
      <li>Well, sump, sewage, or other pump make/model and electrical nameplate data.</li>
      <li>Electric water heater, range, dryer, EV charger, and other large loads you want to run.</li>
      <li>Which loads are essential and which could be delayed, shed, or excluded during an outage.</li>
      <li>Fuel available at the property, panel/service configuration, equipment locations, and any site restrictions.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Photograph the equipment labels and keep the corresponding owner manuals. A general wattage chart is for orientation only; nameplate and installation data take precedence.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="example-loads">
    <h2>Approximate equipment ranges can illustrate why the answer varies</h2>
    <p>Champion Power Equipment's current household chart lists a 3-ton central AC at 3,000–3,500 running watts and 5,000–6,000 starting watts; a 1/2 HP well pump at 750–1,000 running watts and 1,500–2,000 starting watts. These are that manufacturer's generalized reference ranges, not ratings for any particular 2,000-square-foot home. A different appliance label/manual may show substantially different data.</p>
    <p>Use the full <a href="/sizing/appliance-wattage-chart/">appliance wattage chart</a> only to prepare questions. Do not add every starting number and treat the result as a generator recommendation. A professional must check coincident running loads, motor starts, selected circuits, fuel-specific output, transfer equipment, and site conditions.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="professional-assessment">
    <h2>Get a site-specific assessment before choosing capacity</h2>
    <p>Ask the installer to show the exact generator model and its rating on the planned fuel, the selected transfer switch, backed-up circuits, any load-management devices, and the motor-start assumptions. Ask what fuel-line, electrical, panel, placement, permit, and commissioning work is included.</p>
    <p>A current Generac example illustrates why fuel matters: model G0073270 is listed at 26 kW on LP and 24 kW on natural gas. That is the manufacturer rating for that model—not proof that a 26 kW unit fits a particular floor plan. Read our <a href="/sizing/what-size-generator-do-i-need/">load-based sizing guide</a> and the linked model’s current spec sheet, then have the complete system evaluated professionally.</p>
    <p>For cost context, see <a href="/cost/standby-generator-cost/">our standby cost guide</a>; for the installation sequence, see <a href="/installation/what-to-expect/">what to expect during installation</a>.</p>
  </section>
</ArticleLayout>
